INT. HOLLOW OAK TAVERN - NIGHT

A tavern built inside the trunk of a dead oak the size of a cathedral. Lanterns hang from root-beams. The bar is a single slab of petrified heartwood, worn silver by centuries of elbows.

KAELEN MOSSWELL, 20s, a ranger with a bowstring scar across one cheek, nurses a mug at the bar. MOTHER YSOLDE, 70s, a bent innkeeper with moth-wing earrings, wipes the same cup over and over. BRANNOCH, 50s, a barrel-chested dwarf in a miner's leather, sits in the corner with an axe across his lap. PIP, 10, a stable-hand, sweeps near the door. VEYRA, 30s, hooded, a gray cloak pooled around her chair, sits alone with an untouched bowl.

KAELEN
Quiet tonight.

MOTHER YSOLDE
It's always quiet when the root-winds turn.

BRANNOCH
(not looking up)
Quiet's what you get when the road's closed.

Pip stops sweeping. Veyra's spoon clinks against the bowl.

KAELEN
Closed since when?

BRANNOCH
Since someone burned the toll-bridge. Three nights back.

MOTHER YSOLDE
Don't start, Brannoch.

BRANNOCH
I'm only saying what everyone's thinking.

He lifts his eyes toward Veyra. Kaelen follows his gaze. The hood does not move.

KAELEN
(to Veyra)
You came in on the north road. Did you see anything?

VEYRA
Smoke.

KAELEN
Smoke from where?

VEYRA
From the bridge.

PIP
(whispering)
She's lying. The bridge is south.

Brannoch's chair scrapes. His hands tighten on the axe.

BRANNOCH
Speak, hooded one. Who sent you?

VEYRA
No one sent me.

BRANNOCH
Then why is your cloak dry?

Everyone looks at the floor. Rain is pooling under the door, but the gray cloak is perfectly dry, and where its hem touches the boards, small pale flowers are blooming.

MOTHER YSOLDE
Gods in the roots.

Veyra rises. The hood falls back to show eyes like green fire.

VEYRA
The bridge was mine. The road is mine. Everything that grows under this oak is mine.

Brannoch charges. The axe bites the table where she stood, and the tavern erupts in roots.
